    {
      "id": "ef516340-1719-4aae-b5c3-5259f77460a6",
      "name": "Why MCP Is Awesome: Editors Get Superpowers Too",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP Is Awesome: Editors Get Superpowers Too",
      "docTypeId": "35628c7c-7964-4d3c-9be5-0e79c49411d1",
      "templateId": "0347d920-8894-443a-8ea9-94f6cf19ac47",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP Is Awesome: Editors Get Superpowers Too"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-05 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "MCP is not just a developer's toy. When a CMS speaks MCP, content editors can model schema, manage media and publish pages from the tools they already love."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "353cf296-7395-4b51-aef1-00ba02427f36",
              "mediaKey": "a544ef86-937b-4e0c-a712-f4657a297617",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "22cee6d0-a523-40f7-acc1-b31d84473049",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "b4abb546-948d-4823-9715-6672888073a3",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "797a29ba-835a-4a95-94c6-9c0105051d19",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "c59baca7-2448-42ef-aa07-43814795bb93",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "ed82bac2-3ea8-427e-ba62-41f7c2032f10",
                "key": "22cee6d0-a523-40f7-acc1-b31d84473049",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Developers were the first to feel the magic of MCP, but content editors are quietly getting the biggest upgrade. When a CMS exposes itself over MCP, the backoffice stops being the only place where content work happens.</p><p>Imagine an editor sitting in their favourite writing tool, asking an assistant to draft a page, pick images from the media library, translate the copy and hit publish. The assistant calls the same MCP tools a human would click through — with the same permissions, the same validation and the same audit trail.</p><p>That is the superpower: editors keep their workflow, and the CMS keeps its rules. Nobody has to learn a new interface, because the interface they already use now understands the CMS.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "8244f782-cba8-4bd4-ae19-385d177277b7",
                "key": "b4abb546-948d-4823-9715-6672888073a3",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "58ea1b23-7253-442b-b557-ffb57f78ec50",
                        "mediaKey": "b301f2e0-b3ba-46d6-a751-09c757983d0c",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "One connected structure instead of a tangle of cables. Photo by Alina Grubnyak on Unsplash."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "ed82bac2-3ea8-427e-ba62-41f7c2032f10",
                "key": "797a29ba-835a-4a95-94c6-9c0105051d19",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The front page you are reading was assembled by an MCP-driven pipeline: a block grid of rich text, images and quotes, published under a domain, rendered by Razor views that follow Umbraco's own conventions. Every step — schema, media, content, publishing — happened through tools, not through hand-edited files.</p><p>For teams, that means reviewable, repeatable, scriptable content operations. A new site? Model it once over MCP and every environment can be rebuilt from the same calls. A new post every week? The assistant does the typing; the editor does the judging.</p><p>MCP is awesome because it turns the CMS from a walled garden into a well-lit workshop — and hands the editors the keys.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "d6dd18e9-57af-4f5d-af6f-199e4bd6fc33",
                "key": "c59baca7-2448-42ef-aa07-43814795bb93",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "The best interface is the one you already have."
                  },
